<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raja Ravi Varma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ravi Varma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> Varma was an Indian painter, born at Kilimanoor palace in the princely state of Travancore, whose works fused European academic art with Indian iconography. Closely related to the royal family of Travancore, he received the title Raja from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a>. He won acclaim after an award at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1873 and took three gold medals at the World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago in 1893. He painted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> deities and scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, such as Nala and Damayanti. In 1894 he started a lithographic press in Ghatkopar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, whose affordable oleographs carried his images to ordinary people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raja-ravi-varma.jpg" alt="Raja Ravi Varma" width="287" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bhawani Ram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ravivarma1b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ravi Varma was born on 29 April 1848 into an aristocratic family that for over 200 years had produced consorts for the princesses of the matrilineal Travancore royal family. His father, Ezhumavil Neelakanthan Bhattatiripad, was a scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayurveda">Ayurveda</a>. His mother, Uma Ambabayi Thampurratti, was a poet whose Parvati Swayamvaram he published after her death. His brother Raja Varma was also a painter and worked closely with him all his life. In 1866, at the age of 18, he married Bhageerthi Bayi of the royal house of Mavelikkara, which tied him closely to the royal family; the couple had five children. In 1900 two of his granddaughters were adopted into the royal house of Travancore, and the present royal family descends from him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Patronised by Ayilyam Thirunal, the next <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Travancore, Varma began formal training. He learned the basics of painting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, studied water painting under Rama Swami Naidu and, rather reluctantly, oil painting under the British portraitist Theodore Jenson. The British administrator Edgar Thurston helped promote his career and his brother's. He travelled throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in search of subjects and often modelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> goddesses on Indian women whom he considered beautiful. He is particularly noted for paintings of Dushyanta and Shakuntala and of Nala and Damayanti. Critics have called his style showy and sentimental, but his work remains very popular, and many of his paintings are at Laxmi Vilas Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vadodara">Vadodara</a>.</p><p>On the advice, apparently, of the Travancore Dewan T. Madhava Rao, he started the press at Ghatkopar in 1894 and moved it to Malavli near Lonavala in 1899. Its oleographs of gods and goddesses, adapted mainly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, sold in thousands. Managed by Raja Varma, the press was a commercial failure and was sold in 1901 to the German technician Fritz Schleicher, who later employed other artists and added commercial labels. A fire destroyed the factory in 1972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Around his 57th birthday Varma announced that he would take sanyasa at sixty. He suffered grief at the death of Raja Varma and also diabetes, and died on 2 October 1906. In 1904 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a> had awarded him the Kaisar-i-Hind Gold Medal. Geeta Kapur called him the father figure of modern Indian art, and Gulam Mohammed Sheikh also saw him as a key figure, while Ratan Parimoo dismissed his work as kitsch. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>'s government gives the Raja Ravi Varma Puraskaram each year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raja Ravi Varma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
